.TH CVTTEST 1
.SH NAME
cvttest \- test that converting a string to a number keeps all of it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cvttest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cvttest
checks that
.BR "int s" ,
.B "big s"
and
.B "real s"
in Limbo convert the whole of a string's value, in particular above
2\u31\d and 2\u32\d and below \-2\u32\d.
.PP
Each case prints
.B ok
or
.BR FAIL ,
and the last line is a count.
.PP
.B "Run it on a native kernel."
Hosted it passes whether or not the defect it was written for is
present, because the defect is a
.B kencc
one:
.B int s
is the Dis
.B cvtcw
instruction, a
.B WORD
is 64 bits on every target this tree builds, and
.B libinterp/string.c
reached it through
.IR strtol ().
A kencc
.B long
is 32 bits \(em
.B libkern/strtol.c
names its own ceiling,
.BR "#define LONG_MAX 2147483647L" ,
and returns it on overflow \(em so on a native kernel every value above
2\u31\d\-1 came back as exactly
.BR 2147483647 .
Clang's
.B long
is 64 bits, so the same line was correct hosted and wrong nowhere
anybody was looking.
.PP
.B "A saturation, not a wrap,"
which is the tell. A wrap reads as a sign or a width problem in the
code under suspicion; a value pinned to a round constant points at a
.B library
ceiling, and 2147483647 appears in exactly one place in this tree's own
sources.
.SH "HOW IT WAS FOUND, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS"
Not here.
.IR Tkscenetest (1)
serialises a render\-node tree and reads an
.B rgba
of 2425392895 back out of it.
.IP
.EX
host      rgba survives above 2^31: 2425392895
Pi 3B     rgba survives above 2^31: 2147483647
.EE
.PP
Nothing in the conversion family had a test, so a defect in the one
instruction a Limbo program uses to read a number was found by a
graphics test on a different machine. That is what this file is for.
.SH "WHY EVERY NUMBER IS BUILT AT RUNTIME"
.I Limbo
.B folds
a conversion of a constant string, and refuses to compile a fold it
cannot do:
.B "int (\(dq12\(dq + \(dqx\(dq)"
is
.B "digit 'x' not radix 10"
at compile time, not a 12 at run time. So a case written as
.B "int \(dq2425392895\(dq"
tests
.IR limbo (1),
which runs on the host either way, and reaches the VM not at all. Every
number here goes through
.I digits()
or
.IR runtime (),
neither of which the compiler can see through.
.SH "THE CONTROLS"
Three small values first \(em 42, 0 and \-7 \(em because a conversion
that was simply broken would otherwise pass the interesting cases by
accident. They earned themselves immediately: the first version built
its expected digits with
.BR "string c" ,
which appends the character's decimal
.B value
rather than the character, so
.B "string '4'"
is
.B 52
and every case failed. The three controls said so on the first three
lines.
.SH "WHY BOTH SIDES OF EVERY CASE ARE STRINGS"
Because the first version's were not, and it cost a clean pass on a
kernel where three of its numbers were wrong.
.PP
It compared
.I sprint
of the converted value against the digits of a
.B big
.BR literal .
When the literal was itself wrong, both sides were wrong together:
.IP
.EX
ok   int of an rgba above 2^31: \-1869574401
ok   int at 2^32\-1: \-1
ok   int below \-2^32: \-99
.EE
.PP
.B "15 ok, 0 fail,"
on a kernel where three of the fifteen numbers were not the numbers
named in the test.
.B "A symmetric fixture cannot see a fault on the path both of its"
.B "sides take,"
and
.B "big LITERAL"
is on both \(em this tree already has the finding that a
.B computed
all\-ones and a
.B literal
all\-ones are different numbers in this Limbo, so a literal is not a
safe oracle for exactly this question.
.PP
Now the input is a string
.I limbo (1)
cannot fold and the expectation is the same characters, so the only
arithmetic between them is the conversion under test. It is printed
with
.B %bd
on a
.BR big ,
because
.B %d
formats the low 32 bits and would print the right answer and the wrong
answer alike.
.PP
2\u31\d\-1 is checked
.B as well as
2\u31\d, because a fix that moved the ceiling rather than removing it
would pass both the small cases and the large ones but not those two
together. 2\u32\d\-1 is checked because an unsigned 32\-bit ceiling is
the other plausible place to stop and is not where libkern's is, and a
value below \-2\u32\d because
.B LONG_MIN
is a different constant from
.B LONG_MAX
and testing one direction would have missed it.
.PP
.B "big s"
is a different instruction
.RB ( cvtcl )
and already used
.IR strtoll ,
so it is the control that says the two conversions
.B agree
rather than that both are wrong the same way.
.SH "WHAT IT READS NOW"
On the Pi 3B kernel, which is the run that means anything:
.IP
.EX
CVTTEST 17 ok, 0 fail
int of an rgba above 2^31: 2425392895
int of 2^32\-1: 4294967295
int of below \-2^32: \-4294967395
int of 2^63\-1: 9223372036854775807
int of \-2^63: \-9223372036854775808
.EE
.PP
The values are printed because the verdict was not enough once
already: the version before this one answered
.B "15 ok, 0 fail"
on the same kernel with three of its fifteen numbers wrong.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cvttest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR zdivtest (1),
.IR bndstest (1),
.IR tkscenetest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B ICVTCW
is a punt in the arm64 JIT
.RB ( libinterp/comp-arm64.c ),
so there is one implementation and both execution paths are covered by
one fix \(em unlike the zero divide, where the interpreter and the JIT
each needed their own check and a fix to one was recorded as a fix to
both. Worth running under
.B -c0
and
.B -c1
anyway, because that is a property of the current code generator and
not a guarantee.
.PP
It does not cover
.B hex
or any base but 10:
.B int s
in Limbo is decimal only, and the
.I base
argument of the call underneath it is not reachable from Limbo.
.PP
It does not cover a string whose value does not fit a 64\-bit
.BR WORD .
.I Strtoll
saturates there and nothing says what Limbo should do, so asserting
either answer would be inventing a specification.
.PP
.B "There is no in-process negative control here."
The defect cannot be made to appear on the host, so the only evidence
this test can fail is the measurement that found the bug \(em the same
rgba reading 2147483647 on an unfixed Pi 3B kernel and 2425392895
hosted. Running this on a kernel built before
.B libinterp/string.c
changed is the control, and it is a kernel build rather than a flag.
